concepts.content:
From a light source, a dense array of light rays emerges in all directions. Before these light rays reach the eye, the light interacts with the objects in the world. If a light ray is not directed toward the eye, it strikes some surface and, as a result, a new set of rays is emitted in many directions. This process continues, producing more interactions and filling the space. In the middle of the space, an observer senses only a subset of the light rays that strike the eye. As a result of this complex pattern of interactions, even a single ray will form a complex image in the eye of the observer.

@fig-lightRay shows some pictures taken when illuminating a complex scene with a laser pointer that illuminate the scene with a narrow beam of light (this is the best approximation to the image produced by a single light ray that we could do at home).

![(a) Scene illuminated with a ceiling lamp. (b-c) Two images obtained by illuminating a scene with a laser pointer (the red line indicates the direction of the ray).](figures/taxonomy/lightGray2.png){#fig-lightRay}

The resulting images show the complexity of the interactions between the different surfaces. The sum of the contribution of all the light rays emitted by the light source will give rise to a natural looking picture. On each image, the approximate direction of the light beam produced by pointer is indicated by the red arrow in @fig-lightRay (b-c).

